<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../Graphics/Australia_20050921.jpg"><img class="PicBorder" src="Graphics/AustraliaSmall_20050921.jpg" align="right"></a>
<u>04-SEP-2005 9:27 PM (AUS Time)</u><br>
Cairns has a novel concept that lets anyone who has never scuba dived before to dive up to two times.  I had never gotten around to getting my PADI certification back home and I had originally expected to just snorkel the Great Barrier Reef, but when I heard about these so-called introductory dives, I had to jump at the chance.  You see, I'm starting to become a thrill seeker.
<p>
I went with a middle-range reef operator called Passions of Paradise.  They travel via a catamaran.  M couldn't swim so I had to make sure to get her a glass-bottom boat tour and these guys had it.
<p>
We  met the cat at the Marlin Jetty and she was a fine modern boat.  We boarded her and had our morning coffee.  The boat had an abundant amount of hot, sexy women from either here in AUS or Europe.  Then I got to live every heterosexual man's dream as all these beauties unveilved bikini-clad bodies to me.  Anyone remember that scene in National Lampoon's Christmas Vacation where Chevy Chase is staring out the window at the girl in the swimsuit  It was something like that.
<p>
We arrived at the first reef called Hastings Reef near the Breaking Waters area.  SCUBA instruction would be given in 4 groups and I was in the 4th group, so I decided to go snorkeling until it was my turn.  I asked the instructor how cool the water was and he said about 21 degC and suggested a wetsuit, so I put one on.  
<p>
30 minutes later, they called my group number.  Still in my wetsuit, I was being strapped into an oxygen tank and my regulator was turned on.  We practiced breathing through the regulator, how to remove air from our masks, and how to purge water caught in our regulator.  And then over and over again, we repeated the most important rule of SCUBA diving -- BREATHE!
<p>
Now it was time to jump in the water.  As I got up, I could feel the full weight of the tank--pretty heavy!  They strapped a weight belt around me to make sure that will stay level in the water.  As I got to the end of the wooden platform, I took a step off and stepped into the water.  The weight on my back was fully gone since air was lighter than water.  I grabbed onto a rope and the instructor tested my breathing and purging exercises again.  Once he gave me the ok, I descended about 3 ft down and he tested for any problems with pressure buildup in my ears.  I gave him the ok and a thumbs down, which in this case meant that I was ready to dive.  Another SCUBA instructor locked arms with me and another diver!
<p>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1126438469_1.jpg>-->
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1126438469_1.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1126438469_1.jpg"></a>
<p>
We descended to the floor of the ocean near the reef ~60m.  It was a completely different world from this point onwards.  There were things I had never seen or done during my previous snorkeling excursions.  We were able to pick up things, spot fish not near the water surface, touch plant life. We held starfish and spotted NEMO fish (clownfish).  I had experienced the Great Barrief Reef in a completely new way.  
<p>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1126441728_1.jpg>-->
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1126441728_1.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1126441728_1.jpg"></a>
<p>
Time was up and we surfaced.  Getting out with the heavy tank was a huge strain.  If you weren't careful, you could fall in backwards.  Removing everything except for my swimming trunks, we had our lunch while we went to the 2nd dive spot -- Upollo Cay.  Here, the depth of the ocean was quite shallow and I was wondering if the 2nd dive would be a waste of money?  In the near distance was an island made of sand. People could snorkel to there, get out of the water, and walk around.
<p>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1126438469_3.jpg>-->
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1126438469_3.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1126438469_3.jpg"></a>
<p>
This time around there were only 4 of us diving.  Re-geared, we got back in te water, this time not locking arms.  We followed the instructor around and spotted a stingray under a rock and an octopus changing colors inside a cave.  Turtles had been spotted in the water before but we had no such luck.  We had picked up a plant that stuck itself on you.   Pretty cool.  Well it was time to surface and head home.  On the way home, we were fortunte enough to set the sails and cut the motor.  The SCUBA adventure is one I won't forget for a long time.
<p>
